    <item>
      <title>Take Me to Your Leader</title>
      <link>https://darkestnotes.org/posts/take-me-to-your-leader/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 13 Apr 2009 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://darkestnotes.org/posts/take-me-to-your-leader/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&#34;6Ac7wjrdupo2gmotQsHXZbV5o1_500&#34; loading=&#34;lazy&#34; src=&#34;https://darkestnotes.org/posts/take-me-to-your-leader/images/6Ac7wjrdupo2gmotQsHXZbV5o1_500.jpg&#34;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Defense drills at Leningrad, 1937.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Bulla’s photograph of hundreds of children wearing gas masks was not meant to be ghoulish, a commentary on war or lost innocence, but rather exemplified a reason for pride–the country was blessed with well-trained, well-equipped and obviously courageous young fighters.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
